This minimal source-side cleanup is for a future kernel rebuild. It keeps the
successful BW160 channel normalization and lowers its routine diagnostics from
WARN to INFO. With the driver's default RLM mask 0x03 (ERROR + WARN), these
messages then stay quiet unless INFO logging is explicitly enabled.

Not applied to the running kernel. The active fix uses /proc/net/wlan/dbg_level
and the systemd unit documented in ../diagnostics/2026-11-17-filter-fix.txt.

Source: https://github.com/striker91n/ps4-linux-7.3/blob/main/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76x8/drv_wlan/MT6632/wlan/mgmt/rlm.c

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76x8/drv_wlan/MT6632/wlan/mgmt/rlm.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76x8/drv_wlan/MT6632/wlan/mgmt/rlm.c
@@
-			DBGLOG(RLM, WARN, "S1=%d, S2=%d\n", *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS1, *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS2);
+			DBGLOG(RLM, INFO, "S1=%d, S2=%d\n", *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS1, *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS2);
@@
-				DBGLOG(RLM, WARN, "[BW160][new spec] S1 change to %d\n", *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS1);
+				DBGLOG(RLM, INFO, "[BW160][new spec] S1 change to %d\n", *pucVhtChannelFrequencyS1);
